meta {
  name: 37 PDF Report Templates
  seq: 42
}

docs {
  # PDF Report Templates

  Classic HTML-`meta` templates (Header / Body / Footer) rendered to PDF via
  Puppeteer. Two cross-cutting behaviours to note:

  ## Reusable image tags (across templates)
  Images added through "Add Image" upload to S3 and their `{{image:<id>}}` token
  is stored in the template's `meta.images`. On **Create / Update** every
  `meta.images` entry is also written to a durable, tenant-wide registry
  (`print_template_images`). At **generate** time, any `{{image:<id>}}` token that
  is referenced in the header/body/footer but missing from the template's own
  `meta.images` is resolved from that registry. Net effect: a token copied from
  one template renders the same image when pasted into ANOTHER template
  (Header, Body, or Footer). Registry rows are never removed when a template is
  deleted/edited, so an image referenced elsewhere stays resolvable. There is no
  separate upload endpoint — reuse rides on the existing `POST /uploads/attachment`
  flow. Global (SITE_ADMIN) templates use the same mechanism with `tenant_id NULL`.
  Precedence at render: runtime `context.images` > registry > template `meta.images`.

  ## Repeating header/footer on every page (pinned to the edges)
  The Header and Footer are rendered via Puppeteer's native page header/footer
  (`displayHeaderFooter`), so they repeat on EVERY page of a multi-page report and
  are pinned to the physical TOP and BOTTOM edges — even on a short/last page whose
  body doesn't fill it. The `meta.margin_top` / `meta.margin_bottom` values reserve
  the bands the header/footer occupy, so the body never overlaps them. Because
  Chromium renders these bands in an isolated context, each header/footer template
  carries its own `<style>` (base + `custom_css`) and an explicit font size, and
  images use absolute (S3) URLs. Size the top/bottom margins to fit your
  header/footer height (letterhead convention) or tall content will be clipped.
}
